Perfumery in London in 1784. Brands such as Crosse & Blackwell and Sharwood's were established in the 18th and 19th centuries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-curry-powder"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry_powder"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Riz Casimir","dish/riz-casimir"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Banana","ingredient/banana"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]}]},"dish/shengjian-mantou":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/shengjian-mantou","n":"Shengjian mantou","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Shengjian mantou, shengjian bao, or shengjian for short, are small, pan-fried baozi (steamed buns) which are a specialty of Suzhou and Shanghai. The buns are typically filled with pork and gelatin that melts into liquid or soup when cooked. Within Shanghai, shengjian mantou typically have thin, crisp skins, while those sold elsewhere usually have thicker, bread-like skins. The buns originated and became popular in Suzhou at the beginning of the 20th century;  their popularity spread to the Yangtze River Delta. They have been one of the most common breakfast items in Shanghai since the early 1920s."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-shengjian-mantou"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shengjian_mantou"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Gelatin","ingredient/gelatin"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","ref":"site/thewoksoflife-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true},{"n